WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 97,

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.820
coming to you on June 10th, 2026.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.820
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.220
A galactic wind driven by early universe collisions

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.860
is stripping star-forming fuels so fast

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.120
and may explain why so many massive galaxies went dead.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.140
Astronomers detected a powerful outflow

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.660
triggered by galaxy collisions

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.560
that rapidly removed gas needed for new stars.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.320
The wind leaves massive galaxies

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.600
on the brink of becoming permanently dead,

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.020
addressing why more such quiescent systems appear

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.220
than models predicted.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.380
Observations show the mechanism operates

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.860
through repeated mergers that inject energy

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.040
and drive material outward at high speed.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.820
This process offers a direct way to quench star formation

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:50.160
without waiting for slower internal exhaustion of fuel.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.440
Follow-up spectra from larger samples will test

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.200
how common the effect was across cosmic time.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.660
If the Milky Way were scaled to the size of a dinner plate,

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.400
a typical early universe galaxy collision

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.840
would span a city block and release energy equivalent

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.700
to billions of supernovae detonating at once.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.980
The resulting wind slams into surrounding gas

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.140
at thousands of kilometers per second,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.200
sweeping up and heating material

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.800
until it can no longer collapse into new stars.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.720
Telescopes have measured outflow speeds

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.100
exceeding 1,000 kilometers per second

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.240
in galaxies seen when the universe was less than 2 billion years old.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.740
These winds can clear an entire galaxy's reservoir

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.780
of star forming fuel in just a few tens of millions of years.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.260
Yet we still do not know exactly

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.240
how much of the expelled gas falls back later

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.160
versus escaping forever into intergalactic space.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.900
Now shifting to future telescope capabilities.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.540
A new study modeled how the planned

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:53.840
and NASA telescope would detect atmospheric signs of life

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.980
on a planet resembling Earth billions of years ago.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.420
The analysis focused on ozone, oxygen,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.460
and methane combinations that would stand out

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.100
against the background of an early Earth-like world.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.760
Researchers ran simulations across different viewing angles

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.180
and cloud cover to gauge detection.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.760
The work helps set performance requirements

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.920
for the mission's instruments before hardware is finalized.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.660
Next steps include refining the models

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.620
with additional atmospheric scenarios

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.340
expected from upcoming exoplanet surveys.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.360
On a different note, today marks the birth

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.380
of a key figure in solar physics.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.800
Theoretical astrophysicist Eugene Parker

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.780
was born on this date in 1927 and later proposed

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.260
that the sun continuously emits a stream of charged particles.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.480
His 1958 paper described a supersonic solar wind

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.400
that would reach Earth and the outer planets.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.280
Mariner 2 confirmed the prediction four years later,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.780
validating the first detailed model of the heliosphere.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.420
Parker's framework remains central

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.800
to understanding how the sun shapes space weather

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.880
and protects the solar system from galactic cosmic rays.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.180
Ongoing missions continue to test refinement

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.800
of his original equations.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.620
Turning to commercial developments in orbit,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.520
a Los Angeles startup raised $5 million

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.960
to demonstrate computing hardware in space next year.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.040
The company plans an eventual constellation

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.080
of more than 100,000 orbital data centers

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.540
to serve AI workloads.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.760
The demonstration will test power, thermal

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.760
and communication systems in the radiation environment

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:30.520
above Earth.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.220
Success would open a new commercial use

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.600
for low Earth orbit beyond communications

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.840
and Earth observation.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.660
Additional capital rounds are expected

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.660
when the first hardware reaches orbit.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.900
Next up is an expansion of satellite tracking.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.180
The company deployed a transportable tracking radar

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.820
in the Indo-Pacific region

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.960
to maintain continuous coverage of active spacecraft.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.820
The sensor is designed to follow objects

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:59.540
that change orbit, filling gaps left by fixed installations.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.000
Continuous tracking supports collision avoidance

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.340
and helps operators understand the behavior

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.680
of maneuvering satellites.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.940
The system joins an existing global network

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.700
that already monitors thousands of objects daily.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.100
Further mobile units are planned

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.760
for other high traffic regions,

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.320
shifting to propulsion contracts.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.100
Spanish propulsion startup Arcadia

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.460
signed a contract to supply thrusters

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.140
for a satellite under construction

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.560
by Reflex Aerospace.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.440
The engines will enable orbit adjustments

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.140
and end of life disposal for the new spacecraft.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.720
Integration testing is scheduled ahead

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.040
of the planned launch window.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.020
The deal marks another European commercial customer

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.900
for the small thruster specialist.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.220
Additional contracts are under discussion

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.720
with other satellite builders.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:49.260
Interest is also rising in alternative launch methods.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.880
Crowded land ranges and military concerns

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.120
are prompting renewed study of offshore launch platforms.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.540
Proponents argue that mobile sea platforms

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.020
could ease scheduling pressure

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.460
and provide resilient options during conflicts.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.860
Engineering studies are examining stability,

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.720
safety zones and regulatory pathways for floating pads.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.580
Several commercial firms have begun preliminary design work

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.260
on vessels that could support medium lift vehicles.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.220
A demonstration launch from a converted ship

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:20.800
remains a near-term goal.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:22.940
On the technology side,

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:25.440
NASA is testing a compact radar system.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.120
Engineers at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.980
built a multi-frequency radar

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.720
that fits on a small satellite and probes clouds

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.540
at three simultaneous frequencies

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.680
between 36 and 240 gigahertz.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.240
The instrument targets a wide range

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.100
of droplet and ice particle sizes

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.280
to improve precipitation estimates.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.600
CloudCube will fly on a future CubeSat mission

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.200
to collect data over both land and ocean.

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.280
Early ground tests show the compact design

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.020
maintain sensitivity comparable to larger airborne radars.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:59.860
Flight data will help refine algorithms

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.740
for future operational missions.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.820
Another propulsion advance comes from MIT.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.740
Researchers developed a compact electric propulsion system

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.860
sized for CubeSats that could reach the red planet.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.700
The engine uses a novel propellant and electro geometry

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.360
to achieve higher efficiency

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.220
than current small satellite thrusters.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.860
Laboratory tests demonstrated sustained operation

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.880
at power level suitable for interplanetary trajectories.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.080
Mission designers are now modeling trip times

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:31.620
and payload capacity for a Mars transfer.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.560
A flight demonstration on a near-earth mission

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.220
is the next planned milestone.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.760
Europe is also examining supporting infrastructure.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.920
Satellites now underpin timing, communications,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.080
navigation, and climate monitoring.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.740
Yet Europe lacks sufficient ground systems

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.320
to support growing fleets.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.480
The article examines gaps in data processing,

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:55.160
cybersecurity, and spectrum management

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.780
that could limit future missions.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.660
Policy makers are considering new investment

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.880
in shared infrastructure to keep pace

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:02.980
with commercial growth.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.800
Without upgrades, analysts warn that launch capacity alone

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.180
will not deliver false strategic benefit.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.820
A series of workshops will gather input

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.060
from operators and agencies this summer.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.200
In a human interest development,

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.360
a student has refined search targets.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.080
An anchor of student created a catalog

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.560
that removes 1.75 million stars unlikely

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:26.620
to host detectable civilizations,

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:28.700
leaving a shorter target list.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:31.560
The filtering system uses stellar age,

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.740
metalicity, and galactic position

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:35.160
to prioritize candidates.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.220
The resulting public catalog is intended

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.020
to help radio observatories allocate scarce

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.780
observing time more efficiently.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.820
Early tests show the method reduces false positives

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:48.900
from young or metal poor stars.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.700
Larger surveys will incorporate the list

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:53.440
in upcoming observation cycles.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.840
Turning to a proposed lunar mission,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.760
Japanese researchers designed a compact 10-ray instrument

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:01.860
that could survey the entire moon

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.420
from a single satellite in roughly two years.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.440
The telescope would measure elemental abundances

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.680
across the surface to clarify the moon's formation history.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.580
Current sample return data cover only a few locations,

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:17.160
leaving global questions unanswered.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.060
A small satellite mission could fill that gap

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:22.960
at lower cost than previous orbital spectrometers.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.180
Mission concept studies are now under review

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.820
by Japanese space agencies.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.160
The proposed single satellite X-ray telescope

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.160
from mapping the moon would measure elemental abundances

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.180
across every major terrain in roughly two years.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.740
Current samples from Apollo and Chang-Oh missions

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:42.880
represent only a handful of sites,

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.640
leaving global questions about the lunar mantle

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.160
and crust unresolved.

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.960
By detecting characteristic X-ray fluorescence from orbit,

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.760
the instrument could distinguish between competing models

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:57.020
of how the moon formed after the giant impact.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:00.880
Data would also help identify resource-rich regions

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:02.120
for future exploration.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.600
Could a global elemental map finally settle

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.420
whether the moon's far-side crust differs fundamentally

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:09.540
from the near side?

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:12.440
Next is a major upcoming conference.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.300
The world's largest space science meeting

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:19.020
will gather in Florence from one to nine August, 2026.

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:22.060
Organizers expect thousands of researchers

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:24.560
to present results across planetary science,

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.580
astrophysics, and heliophysics.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.400
The assembly includes dedicated sessions

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.420
on upcoming missions and data analysis techniques.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.200
Early registration opens later this month.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:37.120
The event returns to Europe

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:38.860
after the previous meeting in Busan.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.460
Educators are also updating classroom resources.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.660
Educators in the Globe Mission Earth Project

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:47.760
worked with NASA Langley to improve

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.160
how students use satellite landcover data

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:53.060
from MODIS, Landsat, and Sentinel-2.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.860
The community of practice developed new classroom activities

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.720
that match student observations with orbital measurements.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.060
The effort aims to strengthen links

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.260
between ground-based citizen science

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.540
and satellite records.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:08.380
Participating schools tested the materials

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:11.500
during the 2025 to 2026 academic year.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.280
Revised modules will be released for wider use this fall.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.620
Finally, a look at one of Jupiter's moons.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.620
The outermost of Jupiter's four large moons

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:24.100
shows an ancient heavily cratered surface

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:26.140
that has changed little since formation.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:29.580
Its distance from the planet reduces tidal heating

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.560
compared with Europa or Io,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.680
preserving a record of early solar system impacts.

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:38.600
Spacecraft images reveal bright icy planes

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.180
interrupted by dark crater terrain.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.260
Future missions could measure its interior structure

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:46.720
to test whether a subsurface ocean exists.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.220
Calistos stability makes it a key reference point

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.820
for understanding the Jovian systems evolution.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:55.700
Keep an eye on the next round of funding announcements

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.400
for orbital data centers,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.520
as follow-on tests could determine

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.620
whether the concept moves beyond the initial demonstration.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.660
That covers today's Space and Science News.

